 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(V \times S)$, where $V$ is the number of voxels in the light map volume and $S$ is the number of ray march steps (`light_sample_count` or `max_iteration`).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the output 3D texture. Input textures also consume memory.
-*   **Performance:** Performance depends heavily on `light_map_resolution` and `light_sample_count`. The method is executed on the GPU via compute shaders.
-*   **Strengths:** Precomputes complex volumetric lighting and shadows, allowing real-time rendering to simply sample the 3D texture. Efficient for static scenes or scenes with static lighting.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation time. Cannot handle dynamic lights or dynamic geometry changes without recomputation. Resolution is limited by the 3D texture size.
-*   **When to use:** Static volumetric scenes where lighting does not change frequently.
-*   **When not to use:** Dynamic scenes with moving lights or geometry.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the light map volume and $S$ is the number of samples per ray (`light_sample_count`). The loop runs up to `max_iteration` (256) times, but typically terminates earlier if boundaries are hit or if the light intensity decays sufficiently.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the output light map texture. Input textures (density, noise, mask) also consume memory.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales linearly with volume resolution and sample count. The use of a compute shader allows for parallelization across voxels.
+*   **Strengths:** Decouples lighting calculation from rendering time; allows for complex volumetric shadows and god rays effects; efficient for static volumes.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation; static volumes only (unless recomputed); limited dynamic range due to R8 format; sensitive to threshold parameters.
+*   **When to use:** Static volumetric scenes where lighting does not change frequently; when real-time ray marching is too expensive.
+*   **When not to use:** Dynamic volumes with changing density; scenes requiring high dynamic range lighting; real-time scenarios where precomputation time is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-This method is described in the context of a GPU-based volumetric smoke simulation project [S1, unofficial]. It utilizes a compute shader technique to simulate light attenuation through a volumetric medium. The approach combines ray marching with density thresholding to filter noise and enhance visual quality (god rays).
+This method originates from the `VolumetricSmoke` project by furkanunsal42, which implements a GPU-based volumetric smoke simulation and rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader family of techniques for local illumination in participating media, specifically focusing on precomputed light transport to optimize rendering performance.
 
